# Code owners — who GitHub asks for review, by path.
#
# ## What this file does today, and what it does not
#
# It routes review requests. It does **not** block a merge: branch protection on `main` does not have
# *Require review from Code Owners* switched on, and that is deliberate rather than pending.
#
# Turning it on would deadlock this repository. GitHub does not permit anyone to approve their own pull
# request; this repository has one human; `enforce_admins` is on and has no exemption for him. Requiring
# a code owner's approval would therefore mean requiring an approval nobody present is able to give, and
# nothing would ever merge — the same arithmetic that produced the "0 required approving reviews"
# decision in .portulan/proposals/0001-platform-floor-on-main.md.
#
# So this file is a routing table and a statement of ownership, and the sentence that matters is the one
# it does NOT let anyone write: `docs/vision.md` is still protected by prohibition rather than by the
# platform. Switch the setting on when a second reviewer exists — that is the change that makes this file
# a rail, and it is a repository-settings change, which is Gated (.portulan/gate-map.md).
#
# Syntax note: patterns follow gitignore rules, last matching pattern wins, and a pattern with no owner
# clears ownership for those paths. An owner is `@username`, `@org/team-name`, or an email address on
# that account — nothing else. A bare organisation name is not an owner, and neither is a person's name.
#
# **The owner is a team, not a person**, and that is the point of it. `@sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers`
# has one member today and this repository has one human, so nothing about *who reviews* changes. What
# changes is what the day of the second reviewer costs: adding them to the team, rather than editing
# every line below. This file stops needing to know who the people are — the same separation between
# mechanism and policy the product is built on, applied to the one file most likely to name individuals.
#
# It was created in the order that is not optional, because each step failing is silent rather than
# loud: the team exists, its privacy is `closed` (**visible** — a `secret` team is refused as a code
# owner), it holds **write** on this repository, and only then did this file point at it. Verified by
# GitHub itself rather than by eye — `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/codeowners/errors` returns zero — which
# matters because **an invalid line is skipped, not rejected**: the paths it named end up with no owner
# while the file still reads as complete. That is the quietest way for this file to be useless.
#
# Membership is the thing to keep true from here. A team with no members owns every path and notifies
# nobody, and the file would look exactly as it does now.

# Everything, by default.
*                       @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers

# The constitution. Human-owned, never edited by an agent at all — see .portulan/gate-map.md,
# "Above the tiers". This line does not enforce that; it records who must be asked.
/docs/vision.md         @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers

# The living plan: milestone status and the session log are the build's own record.
/docs/plan.md           @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers

# The engine and the contract other people's workspaces are validated against. A change here reaches
# every installation, which is what makes it worth naming separately from the default.
/core/                  @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers
/spec/                  @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers

# Packaging and distribution: the marketplace manifest, the plugin manifest, and the release surface.
/.claude-plugin/        @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers
/plugin/                @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers

# The gates themselves. A change that relaxes a check is the one worth scrutinising hardest, because it
# is the change that makes every future green mean less.
/.github/               @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers
/.portulan/verify/      @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers
/.portulan/gate-map.md  @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers
/CODEOWNERS             @sleepy-panda-srl/maintainers
